  "research": {
    "area-3": {
      "text": "This \"lifestyle centre\" is described as a mixed-use development with retail, dining, and entertainment, and is undergoing expansion with new residential units and retail spaces.\n\nRecent zoning changes in Toronto aim to allow small shops and cafes in some residential neighborhoods, but this is subject to specific criteria, such as corner lots, proximity to parks or schools, or abutting commercially zoned lots.\n\nThe proposed concept, which includes an indoor playground, would likely be classified as a \"recreation use\" or \"amusement establishment\" by the city, which has distinct zoning and permitting requirements.\n\nThese types of businesses typically need commercial zoning, and a change of use permit would likely be required if the space was previously for retail or warehouse.\n\nOther cafes with indoor playgrounds in Toronto, such as The Willow Play Cafe and Pinocchio Play Café, offer a mix of cafe items and play areas for children.\n\nKira Kids Cafe also features refreshments and snacks that are nut-free.\n\nThe Shops at Don Mills offers on-site parking in surface lots and a multi-level parkade.\n\nThe area also has public transit routes.\n\nThe Shops at Don Mills is described as a \"lifestyle centre\" with a network of internal streets, creating a pedestrian-friendly atmosphere.\n\nInvestigate the available floor area and ceiling height, as these are critical for an indoor playground.\n\nConfirm the number of exits and washrooms to understand the potential occupant load under the Ontario Building Code and Fire Code.\n\nInquire about the process for obtaining a change-of-use permit, a building permit for any alterations, and a business license for an \"amusement establishment\" from the City of Toronto, as well as health permits if food is served.",
